          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The urgency check found no emergency or urgent pattern in its red-flag table. That is not a clearance. The table covers only a fixed list of presentations, so a serious problem can still fall outside it. The sleep-trouble description did not match any body-system pattern in the tools.\n\n**How the guidance describes this:** The insomnia reference says chronic insomnia disorder means difficulty falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early, at least three nights a week for at least three months, with daytime effects. Your three months could meet that duration, but I can't tell from your message how often it happens or how it affects your days. A clinician can work that out with you.\n\n**Red flags to seek prompt care for:** The heart failure reference lists waking at night short of breath, and breathlessness when lying flat, as signs that need medical attention. Get care soon if you have either of these, or new or worsening swelling in your legs or ankles. The iron-deficiency reference lists restless legs and unusual fatigue or breathlessness on exertion as possible signs of low iron. Mention these to a clinician if they apply to you.\n\n**Causes you asked about:** I could not check these. The tool-call limit ran out before I could search the material on stress, mood disorders, sleep apnea, medication or substance effects, and other medical conditions. I won't fill that gap from memory. Please ask a clinician to go through these with you. A good first step is a visit to discuss the three-month pattern, including what your nights look like, any medicines or substances you use, and any breathing, mood, or daytime changes you've noticed.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
